    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-70032-y",
    "abstract": "How fast future sea level rises will depend on the Antarctic Ice Sheet (AIS) response to warming. AIS projections are shaped by the assumption that sea-level peaks during past interglacials occurred after the North American ice sheet complex (NAIS) disappeared. We synthesize evidence from paleoceanography and allied disciplines to argue that NAIS persisted into some of the warmest interglacials of the last million years. We show that overlooking NAIS persistence may lead to underestimation of AIS sensitivity to warming, and propose that this paradigm shift opens research avenues that can increase confidence in the accuracy of climate and sea-level projections.",
    "field": "Paleoclimatology",
    "problem": "Existing sea-level projections are based on the potentially flawed assumption that the North American ice sheet disappeared before past interglacial peaks, leading to an underestimation of the Antarctic Ice Sheet's sensitivity to warming.",
    "method": "The paper proposes synthesizing paleo-evidence to argue for North American ice sheet persistence into past interglacials, which re-evaluates Antarctic Ice Sheet sensitivity to warming and should inform future sea-level projections.",
    "task": "The task is to synthesize evidence for North American ice sheet persistence into past interglacials to argue that this alters estimates of Antarctic Ice Sheet sensitivity and should inform future sea-level projections.",
