    "innovation": "This paper synthesizes evidence to argue that the North American Ice Sheet persisted into past warm interglacials, which implies that the Antarctic Ice Sheet's sensitivity to warming has been underestimated and that future sea-level rise projections should be re-evaluated.",
    "kg_extraction_triplets": [
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "PMIP4",
            "source_entity_type": "Activity",
            "relation": "PART_OF_PROJECT",
            "target_entity_instance": "CMIP6",
            "target_entity_type": "Project",
            "paragraph": "First, recent ocean and atmosphere simulations have modeled the LIG using ice configurations in which the LIS disappeared before 127 ka in part because the Paleoclimate Modeling Intercomparison Project (PMIP) endorsed this timing (Kageyama et al. 2018, Menviel et al. 2019) and made the recommended ice histories available. ... Kageyama, M., Braconnot, P., Harrison, S.P., Haywood, A.M., Jungclaus, J.H., Otto-Bliesner, B.L., Peterschmitt, J.-Y., Abe-Ouchi, A., Albani, S., Bartlein, P.J., Brierley, C., Crucifix, M., Dolan, A., Fernandez-Donado, L., Fischer, H., Hopcroft, P.O., Ivanovic, R.F., Lambert, F., Lunt, D.J., Mahowald, N.M., Peltier, W.R., Phipps, S.J., Roche, D.M., Schmidt, G.A., Tarasov, L., Valdes, P.J., Zhang, Q., Zhou, T., 2018. The PMIP4 contribution to CMIP6 \u2013 Part 1: Overview and over-arching analysis plan. Geoscientific Model Development 11, 1033\u20131057. https://doi.org/10.5194/gmd-11-1033-2018",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": false,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Bipolar Seesaw",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Southern Ocean",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "LIS LIG persistence could also affect AIS and GrIS dynamics via climate mechanisms. Increased AIS melt could have occurred with a persistent LIG LIS via an oceanic teleconnection (Clark et al., 2020). Oceanographic evidence suggests that pulses of cold glacial water, likely of LIS origin, may have affected North Atlantic circulation into the early LIG (Zhang et al. 2021). North Atlantic circulation modifications of this kind would have warmed the Southern Ocean via the classic Bipolar Seesaw. Southern Ocean warming in turn likely influenced Antarctica by encouraging intrusion of circumpolar deep water onto the continental shelf (Wang et al., 2022).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "North Atlantic circulation",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "North Atlantic",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "LIS LIG persistence could also affect AIS and GrIS dynamics via climate mechanisms. Increased AIS melt could have occurred with a persistent LIG LIS via an oceanic teleconnection (Clark et al., 2020). Oceanographic evidence suggests that pulses of cold glacial water, likely of LIS origin, may have affected North Atlantic circulation into the early LIG (Zhang et al. 2021). North Atlantic circulation modifications of this kind would have warmed the Southern Ocean via the classic Bipolar Seesaw. Southern Ocean warming in turn likely influenced Antarctica by encouraging intrusion of circumpolar deep water onto the continental shelf (Wang et al., 2022).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "8.2 ka proglacial lake flood",
            "source_entity_type": "Weather_Event",
            "relation": "IMPACTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Labrador Sea",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Multiple lines of evidence suggest that this layer has the same geographic origin as a red layer of ~8.2 ka age deposited when a proglacial lake flooded under then burst through a remnant Laurentide Ice Sheet (LIS) ice dam to enter the Labrador Sea (Kerwin, 1996; Jennings et al., 2015; Brouard et al., 2021, Figure 2a).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "LIG outburst flood event",
            "source_entity_type": "Weather_Event",
            "relation": "IMPACTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "West Antarctica",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Finally, the timing of LIG LIS disintegration overlaps with the age (126 ka) of the oldest dateable ice at the Skytrain Ice Rise of West Antarctica's Ronne Ice Shelf (Wolff et al., 2025). The Skytrain Ice Rise abruptly thinned by 400 meters during the 8.2 ka event (Grieman et al., 2024), suggesting a link between the Holocene LIS outburst flood and Weddell Sea ice loss. If an LIG outburst flood event also drove West Antarctic thinning just prior to 126 ka, it could explain the lack of Skytrain Ice Rise ice >126 ka.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Holocene LIS outburst flood",
            "source_entity_type": "Weather_Event",
            "relation": "IMPACTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Weddell Sea",
